WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.704 --> 00:00:13.009
Charles father once told his son, "You will be a disgrace to yourself and all your family," because

2
00:00:13.049 --> 00:00:19.752
Darwin, an apathetic student, seemed only interested in dogs, hunting, and catching rats.

3
00:00:20.412 --> 00:00:27.215
But at 22, when he had the opportunity to join an expedition on the ship Beagle, Darwin's life

4
00:00:27.295 --> 00:00:35.514
changed. One result of that trip was On the Origin of Species, a landmark book published in 1859

5
00:00:35.955 --> 00:00:38.015
that described the theory of evolution.

6
00:00:38.756 --> 00:00:46.739
It was a revolutionary idea, maintaining that each species of every plant and animal gradually evolves from common

7
00:00:46.879 --> 00:00:55.063
ancestors and, over time, develops traits that help it respond to environmental pressures and enables it to survive.

8
00:00:55.843 --> 00:01:01.706
Modern scientists consider this theory one of the most important scientific advances in history.

9
00:01:02.406 --> 00:01:09.609
Darwin was born February 12, 1809, in England, to a prominent physician named Robert Darwin.

10
00:01:10.189 --> 00:01:14.651
His mother died when Darwin, the fifth of six children, was only eight.

11
00:01:15.452 --> 00:01:20.934
His father, now the central figure in his life, was dismayed that his son was not interested in

12
00:01:21.014 --> 00:01:28.389
school, preferring long, solitary walks observing nature and collecting insects, leaves, and rocks.

13
00:01:29.209 --> 00:01:34.710
Later, Darwin attended medical school at Edinburgh University to please his father.

14
00:01:35.411 --> 00:01:41.692
But young Darwin was aghast at the barbarity of the surgery performed there and thought the lectures were

15
00:01:41.712 --> 00:01:44.853
"intolerably dull," so his grades suffered.

16
00:01:45.513 --> 00:01:49.595
Disappointed again by his son's academic indifference, Dr.

17
00:01:49.615 --> 00:01:54.656
Darwin enrolled his son at Cambridge University, hoping he would become a clergyman.

18
00:01:55.372 --> 00:02:02.354
But at Cambridge, Darwin serendipitously met the most important mentor of his life and chose another path.

19
00:02:02.994 --> 00:02:07.015
In the early nineteenth century, collecting beetles became a craze.

20
00:02:07.655 --> 00:02:13.237
College students competed to see who could find a new species of beetle, and young men went on

21
00:02:13.277 --> 00:02:18.558
nature walks hoping to best their fellow collectors by finding rare specimens.

22
00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:26.925
Darwin needed some expert advice to beat his rival, Charles "Beetles" Babington, so he went to John Stevens

23
00:02:26.985 --> 00:02:29.406
Henslow, a professor of botany.

24
00:02:30.007 --> 00:02:34.229
Thus began a relationship between the two men that lasted a lifetime.

25
00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:41.633
It was Henslow who encouraged Darwin to join the Beagle expedition even though Darwin's father was against it,

26
00:02:42.054 --> 00:02:44.355
calling the trip "a wild scheme."

27
00:02:45.075 --> 00:02:53.280
So on December 27, 1831, Charles Darwin set out on a five-year, round-the-world sea voyage.

28
00:02:54.132 --> 00:03:00.556
Although he suffered from acute seasickness in the beginning, the trip was just what Darwin needed, and he

29
00:03:00.616 --> 00:03:04.198
explored and collected specimens wherever the ship anchored.

30
00:03:05.098 --> 00:03:10.001
Most of the trip was spent mapping the South American coastline; although he didn't know it at the

31
00:03:10.061 --> 00:03:16.505
time, the five-week stopover in the Galápagos Islands, off the coast of Ecuador, would prove to be a

32
00:03:16.785 --> 00:03:19.066
pivotal moment in his scientific thinking.

33
00:03:20.107 --> 00:03:28.818
There, Darwin collected, dissected, preserved, and labeled specimens from all branches of zoology and made notes on their

34
00:03:28.878 --> 00:03:32.319
environment, appearance, and even their stomach contents.

35
00:03:33.059 --> 00:03:40.721
When he returned to England in 1836, Darwin carried with him about 3,000 pages of notes and about

36
00:03:40.741 --> 00:03:42.282
5,000 specimens.

37
00:03:43.222 --> 00:03:48.023
Soon Darwin began to ask himself questions about what he had found in South America.

38
00:03:48.744 --> 00:03:56.387
Why, for example, did members of one species isolated on an island have different characteristics from a similar

39
00:03:56.487 --> 00:03:59.248
species on the continent or on another island?

40
00:04:00.028 --> 00:04:07.451
As was popular at the time, Darwin joined scholarly groups such as the Zoological Society and the Geological

41
00:04:07.511 --> 00:04:08.691
Society of London.

42
00:04:09.492 --> 00:04:16.074
As a member, Darwin frequently attended lectures and presented papers to enthusiastic audiences.

43
00:04:16.975 --> 00:04:24.551
In addition, societies were meeting places where he could discuss his scientific findings and, more importantly, his many

44
00:04:24.631 --> 00:04:32.837
questions. To Darwin, it seemed the most important piece of the evolutionary puzzle concerned the bird species he

45
00:04:32.857 --> 00:04:34.958
had collected on the Galápagos Islands.

46
00:04:36.099 --> 00:04:43.844
Birds that Darwin thought were grosbeaks, wrens, and orioles were in fact 13 different species of the same

47
00:04:43.984 --> 00:04:46.065
kind of bird—a finch.

48
00:04:46.906 --> 00:04:53.859
These finches had various types of beaks: some were short and stubby while others were long and pointy.

49
00:04:54.579 --> 00:04:57.582
Other beak types ranged between these two extremes.

50
00:04:58.422 --> 00:05:04.247
Darwin observed that the type of beak seemed to vary according to the type of food available where

51
00:05:04.287 --> 00:05:05.247
the finches lived.

52
00:05:06.028 --> 00:05:13.294
Darwin's theory of evolution started taking shape after he began considering these differences, and he even drew a

53
00:05:13.374 --> 00:05:16.336
sketch of an evolutionary tree in his notebook.

54
00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:24.646
Basically, the theory states that of the offspring produced by a species, only those suited to find food,

55
00:05:25.066 --> 00:05:31.412
mates, and nesting places in their environment will survive to genetically pass on their traits to the next

56
00:05:31.492 --> 00:05:38.837
generation. In an environment where the only food is hard seeds, only birds with beaks that can crush

57
00:05:39.098 --> 00:05:41.399
such seeds are likely to survive.

58
00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:50.825
Evolution takes place as traits are passed down through each generation; eventually, members of one species will gradually

59
00:05:50.905 --> 00:05:53.326
change until a new species develops.

60
00:05:53.867 --> 00:05:58.288
Darwin called this process "evolution through natural selection."

61
00:05:59.209 --> 00:06:05.751
In 1838, Darwin began to write out his ideas, but lacking confidence in his own credentials as a

62
00:06:05.792 --> 00:06:07.732
scientist, did not publish them.

63
00:06:08.493 --> 00:06:14.915
Darwin worried about how his church and his family would react to his new theory, which challenged religious

64
00:06:14.995 --> 00:06:16.576
beliefs in divine creation.

65
00:06:17.364 --> 00:06:21.646
His wife, Emma, the mother of his ten children, was a devout Christian.

66
00:06:22.407 --> 00:06:30.111
Darwin eventually put snippets of his theory into a book, The Voyage of the Beagle, published in 1839,

67
00:06:30.791 --> 00:06:37.495
and in l844, he outlined his theory in a manuscript he later circulated among a few friends.

68
00:06:38.356 --> 00:06:46.180
At the same time, Darwin worked to develop his scientific expertise, publishing on various topics in natural history

69
00:06:46.644 --> 00:06:56.492
and corresponding with scientists such as botanist Joseph Dalton Hooker, geologist Charles Lyell, and Alfred Russel Wallace, a

70
00:06:56.572 --> 00:06:59.574
young, self-educated biologist like himself.

71
00:07:00.475 --> 00:07:07.720
When Darwin learned in 1858 that Wallace was planning to publish his own paper on natural selection, he

72
00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:14.333
realized that (after working far longer on the concept than Wallace) he was about to lose the glory

73
00:07:14.433 --> 00:07:16.673
of being the first to announce the theory.

74
00:07:17.333 --> 00:07:25.636
So Charles Lyell suggested the two men jointly present papers on natural selection in July 1858 at a

75
00:07:25.716 --> 00:07:29.597
meeting of the Linnean Society, a natural history society.

76
00:07:30.197 --> 00:07:32.698
But the papers created little excitement.

77
00:07:33.398 --> 00:07:39.820
When Darwin published On the Origin of Species the next year, however, the book proved to be unexpectedly

78
00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:44.955
popular. But a storm of criticism arose too, as Darwin had feared.

79
00:07:45.816 --> 00:07:52.420
Some of his friends and colleagues defended him and his theory, but others bitterly attacked him.

80
00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:59.585
In 1871, his book The Descent of Man claimed that people were just another species of animal and

81
00:07:59.625 --> 00:08:02.867
that humans and apes evolved from a common ancestor.

82
00:08:03.848 --> 00:08:10.216
Cartoons of Darwin with a long beard and an ape body suddenly appeared in the press, but Darwin

83
00:08:10.297 --> 00:08:16.820
stood by his scientific findings; worrying about public opinion never seemed to be part of his temperament.

84
00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:25.505
Darwin is considered the father of modern biology because he recognized the common evolutionary thread that all living

85
00:08:25.585 --> 00:08:26.305
things share.

86
00:08:27.065 --> 00:08:36.370
His work included 19 books and many scholarly papers, which spanned subjects as diverse as barnacles, coral, the

87
00:08:36.410 --> 00:08:42.234
relationship between plants and their pollinators, and the role of earthworms in creating mold.

88
00:08:42.914 --> 00:08:50.219
Darwin attributed his achievements to his "methodical habits" and his "complex and diversified mental qualities."

89
00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:58.765
He wrote, "Of these the most important have been—the love of science—unbounded patience and a fair share of

90
00:08:58.865 --> 00:09:00.667
invention and commonsense."

91
00:09:01.587 --> 00:09:09.064
He died April 19, 1882, and was buried at Westminster Abbey close to Sir Isaac Newton, the man

92
00:09:09.124 --> 00:09:10.285
who discovered gravity.

93
00:09:10.866 --> 00:09:13.868
Darwin's father would finally have been proud.
